<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scindias</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scindias were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruling family who became one of the most powerful houses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a>, later based at Gwalior. Along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a>, they held large territories in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Rivalry among these chiefs weakened central authority after 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scindias.jpg" alt="Scindias" width="196" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwalior_Coat_of_arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family's ancestors held the hereditary post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patil">Patil</a>, or village head, of Kanherkhed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and in the 15th and 16th centuries had served as shiledars, cavalrymen, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>. Members of the family joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> and his sons Sambhaji and Rajaram. Under Maratha rule the name was spelled Shinde; Scindia, also written Sindhia, is the Indo-Persian form of Sendrak. The house was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranoji-scindia">Ranoji Scindia</a>, who rose from bodyguard to commander under Peshwa Bajirao I, one of several young men without hereditary rights, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-holkar">Malhar Rao Holkar</a> among them, whom Bajirao promoted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ranoji led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1726 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> his capital in 1731. The Scindias held sway over many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states and became a major power in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the later 18th century, collecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a>, a share of revenue, from neighbouring rulers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a> (1768–1794) their territory stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> to Ahmednagar and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> to the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>, and he defeated the British at Wadgaon in the First Anglo-Maratha War. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-rao-scindia">Daulat Rao Scindia</a> fought the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha War</a> of 1818 the family accepted a subsidiary alliance with the British, gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and moved its base from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> to Gwalior, which became a princely state. After Daulat Rao's death Baiza Bai ruled until the adopted Jankoji Rao took charge; when Jankoji died in 1843 his widow Tarabai Raje Scindia adopted Jayajirao. On 29 December 1843 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> forces defeated the Scindia armies at Maharajpur and Punniar, and a treaty of 31 December arranged the occupation of Gwalior fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Scindias ruled Gwalior until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence in 1947, when Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jivajirao-scindia">Jivajirao Scindia</a> acceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>. Gwalior was merged into the new state of Madhya Bharat, which he served as rajpramukh, or appointed governor, from 28 May 1948 to 31 October 1956, when it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. The monarchy was abolished in 1971. The family's career in electoral politics began in 1962, when his widow Vijayraje Scindia was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a>, and several of her descendants have since sat in parliament and state assemblies. Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madho-rao-scindia">Madho Rao Scindia</a> founded two orders of chivalry, the Order of the Snake and the Gwalior Medal, in 1900 and 1907.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Scindia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, <b>Scindias</b> of Gwalior and Bhonsles of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1730s the Marathas under the Peshwa Baji Rao I and later the <b>Scindia</b> and Holkar houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess Krishna Kumari was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Damaji Rao II took Baroda (Vadodara) and much of the province, and the Gaekwads became one of the great Maratha houses alongside the Peshwas, Holkars and <b>Scindias</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li><li><blockquote>Dhar was badly weakened by the wars of <b>Scindia</b> and Holkar around 1800 and recovered under British protection after the treaty of 1819.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhar-state">Dhar State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1806 the British returned Gohad and Gwalior to the <b>Scindias</b> and compensated Rana Kirat Singh with Dholpur, Bari and Rajakhera on the Chambal river.</blockquote><a href="/state/dholpur-state">Dholpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>It paid tribute to the <b>Sindhia</b> rulers of Gwalior State and came under British protection in the early 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/khilchipur-state">Khilchipur State</a></li></ul></section>
